vlm for linux on x86_64

if you don't know what it is, you probably don't want it...

based on the admirable work of Brad Parker who changed the alpha assembler
code generator to produce c-code runnable on an x86_64 platform

The default build now uses the direct Ivory interpreter in c-emulator. It
shares the VLM memory manager, world loader, debugger interface, networking,
and other host services. The generated Alpha interpreter remains available
with -DVLM_C_ENGINE=OFF for comparison.
This implements the virtual Ivory environment used by Genera. Operations
unsupported by the legacy VLM, such as UNIFY, still enter the guest trap path.

this version uses a TUNTAP tap device for networking, that way
it doesn't need root privileges to run if you define a tap
interface beforehand. I decided to use a tap (as opposed to a tun)
interface because access to the complete ethernet frame is possible
with a tap interface - making my life easier since I'm currently
writing a chaosnet network module for Linux and would need access to
the complete ethernet frame therefore. Also it is possible to put tap
devices on a bridge with different mac addresses to be able to run
multiple vlm instances communicating to each other via chaosnet.

this version has fixes for:
     - X11 with xcb lib (runs with or without xcb)
     since clx directly accesses the bar-bones X11-protocol, xcb
     gets confused when you try to XCloseDisplay/XSynch because clx tries
     to keep track of the X11 calls internally which it is unable to
     do with a mixture of XLIB calls and direct access to the X11-protocol.
     This in turn keeps the vlm from cleanly closing a display, which it
     uses when terminating and/or saving a LISP world.
     So, if we detect xcb being used, we use a workaround for closing
     X displays...

     - keyboard mapping for the cold-load/debugger window
     I deemed it unsatisfactory to not being able to use the CTRL-, META-,
     SUPER-, and HYPER- keys in the cold-load/debugger window, so I did setup
     a mapping for those keys like it is done in the GENERA system itself. You
     may use the same keys now in the cold-load/debugger window.

     - rational division (/ 3) yields 1/3 instead of 0
     doesn't work in Brads version, because rational division ought to
     produce a trap if there is a non-zero remainder from a division. I've
     decide to produce asm code for rational division by generating a new
     pseudo alpha instruction (x64ratquo) and generating asm code from that.
     see alpha-assembler/imacmath.lisp and stub/process.lisp

     - double float exceptions (/ 0.0d0) results in a valid trap now
     has not been implemented in the alpha assembly version (forgotten
     perhaps).
     Changed that in the generation of alpha assembly code (tested on an alpha
     as well). see alpha-assembler/imactrap.lisp.

there are extensions for:
      - blocksize settable for attach-disk-channel (to be able to use fep-file
      images with non-2048 word blocks, like images derived from ancient
      ESDI disks.

      - a unix crypt routine
      links to unix crypt to enable use of modern crypt algorithms.

      - unix cwd and unix home dir to be found in embedded communication area

      You won't be able to use these extensions if you do not patch and
      recompile respective the LISP definitions as well.

To build on x86_64 Linux, install GCC, CMake 3.20 or newer, Make or Ninja,
and the development headers and libraries for X11 (including Xlib-xcb) and
libcrypt. Then run:

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build --parallel
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

The executables are build/src/genera and build/src/byteswap_world. Use
./build/src/genera in your existing world-launch command. CMake selects
GNU C99 explicitly because the code uses a word-sized bool typedef and
function declarations that are incompatible with C23. Build products stay
inside the build directory.

The default Release build preserves -Ofast and -march=native. Use
-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug for debugging, -DVLM_FAST=OFF for -O2 in Release,
or -DVLM_NATIVE=OFF to build for other x86_64 CPUs. See INSTALL for all options.

The C engine does not need CLISP or generated interpreter sources. To regenerate
the legacy engine from the Alpha assembler and Lisp macros, configure with
-DVLM_C_ENGINE=OFF -DVLM_REGENERATE_STUBS=ON. Generated C files go into build/stub and are rebuilt
when their inputs change; the checked-in copies are not overwritten.

For an existing build that has cached VLM_C_ENGINE=OFF, reconfigure with
cmake -S . -B build -DVLM_C_ENGINE=ON, then rebuild. The ivory-cpu test exercises
the selected engine without a world image, display, or network device. It
covers arithmetic, memory and register access, calls, APPLY, bindings,
unwind-protect, stack-cache movement, interrupts, and guest trap handlers.

To check block-address registers across garbage collection, load
tests/bar-gc.lisp in a fresh Genera test session. It forces GC between a box's
allocation and initialization, then verifies all four fields. Success prints
"BAR GC regression passed." The test enables ephemeral GC in that session.

To check shutdown with a real world, prefix your normal ./build/src/genera command
with "python3 tests/shutdown.py --". This requires a working X display and TAP
interface, and starts four temporary instances to check declining and confirming
exit for SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP, and SIGQUIT.

If startup reports "Unable to set calendar clock" after you enter the date,
leave TIME:SET-CALENDAR-CLOCK as the debugger's current frame and press Ctrl-R.
At "Return from TIME:SET-CALENDAR-CLOCK? (Y or N)", type Y. The caller does
not need a return value; this skips the failed clock-setting call and lets
initialization continue. Abort would instead exit the initialization process.

Older worlds reject modern years from the host clock. Once Genera can access
the repository files (for example through NFS), load OG2-patches/use-host-time.lisp
inside Genera. This repairs the year decoding/validation and enables startup
from the host clock. Evaluate (time:initialize-timebase) to use it immediately,
then save a new world to retain the patch for subsequent boots. Rebuilding
the emulator alone does not install Lisp patches into an existing world.

To install, follow the instructions given in the file INSTALL.

cmake -LAH -N build lists the configuration options for an existing build.
